2025-05-01 03:28:33 - Thread-1436 (execute) - INFO - [Exploration]
Relevant columns and their potential usage
• payment.payment_date : needed to find the first (earliest) payment for every customer  
• payment.customer_id : customer identifier that ties all customer‑level calculations together  
• payment.amount : contributes to each customer’s total money spent  
• payment.rental_id : bridge from a payment to the corresponding rental  
• rental.rental_id : joins to payment and yields inventory_id  
• rental.inventory_id : bridge from a rental to an inventory row (and therefore to a film)  
• inventory.film_id : identifies the movie that was rented  
• film.film_id : joins to inventory and supplies the movie’s attributes  
• film.rating : the rating category that we ultimately group by  
• rental.customer_id (redundant but handy) : can double‑check the customer in case it’s needed  

Typical conditions that will be used  
1. payment.payment_date = (   SELECT MIN(p2.payment_date) … )      – to locate the first payment per customer  
2. payment.customer_id  = rental.customer_id                     – joins on customer id  
3. payment.rental_id    = rental.rental_id                       – joins payment → rental  
4. rental.inventory_id  = inventory.inventory_id                 – joins rental → inventory  
5. inventory.film_id    = film.film_id                           – joins inventory → film  

Below are ten exploratory SQLite queries (each limited to 20 rows except the final one) that gradually build up to the required answer.

```sql
-- 1. Distinct movie ratings available in the catalog
SELECT DISTINCT rating
FROM film
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2. Earliest payment date for the first 20 customers
SELECT customer_id,
       MIN(payment_date) AS first_payment_date
FROM payment
GROUP BY customer_id
ORDER BY customer_id
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3. First payment rows (one per customer) including the rental_id they paid for
SELECT p.payment_id,
       p.customer_id,
       p.rental_id,
       p.payment_date
FROM payment AS p
JOIN (
        SELECT customer_id,
               MIN(payment_date) AS first_payment_date
        FROM payment
        GROUP BY customer_id
     ) AS fp
  ON p.customer_id = fp.customer_id
 AND p.payment_date = fp.first_payment_date
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4. Link those first rentals to the film and fetch the rating
SELECT p.customer_id,
       f.rating,
       p.payment_id,
       p.payment_date
FROM payment  AS p
JOIN rental   AS r ON r.rental_id    = p.rental_id
JOIN inventory AS i ON i.inventory_id = r.inventory_id
JOIN film      AS f ON f.film_id      = i.film_id
JOIN (
        SELECT customer_id,
               MIN(payment_date) AS first_payment_date
        FROM payment
        GROUP BY customer_id
     ) AS fp
  ON p.customer_id = fp.customer_id
 AND p.payment_date = fp.first_payment_date
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5. Total amount each customer has ever spent
SELECT customer_id,
       SUM(amount) AS total_spent
FROM payment
GROUP BY customer_id
ORDER BY customer_id
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6. Total rentals (distinct rental_id) per customer
SELECT customer_id,
       COUNT(DISTINCT rental_id) AS total_rentals
FROM payment
GROUP BY customer_id
ORDER BY customer_id
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 7. Combine total_spent and total_rentals in a single view
SELECT t.customer_id,
       t.total_spent,
       r.total_rentals
FROM (
        SELECT customer_id,
               SUM(amount) AS total_spent
        FROM payment
        GROUP BY customer_id
     ) AS t
JOIN (
        SELECT customer_id,
               COUNT(DISTINCT rental_id) AS total_rentals
        FROM payment
        GROUP BY customer_id
     ) AS r
  ON t.customer_id = r.customer_id
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 8. Join the customer‑level aggregates to the rating of the customer’s first movie
SELECT a.customer_id,
       f.rating,
       a.total_spent,
       (a.total_rentals - 1) AS subsequent_rentals
FROM (
        SELECT p.customer_id,
               SUM(p.amount)                        AS total_spent,
               COUNT(DISTINCT p.rental_id)          AS total_rentals,
               MIN(p.payment_date)                  AS first_payment_date
        FROM payment AS p
        GROUP BY p.customer_id
     ) AS a
JOIN payment  AS p1 ON p1.customer_id = a.customer_id
                   AND p1.payment_date = a.first_payment_date   -- first payment row
JOIN rental   AS r  ON r.rental_id    = p1.rental_id
JOIN inventory AS i ON i.inventory_id = r.inventory_id
JOIN film      AS f ON f.film_id      = i.film_id
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 9. Aggregate the previous result by rating to get averages
SELECT first_rating       AS rating,
       AVG(total_spent)   AS avg_total_spent,
       AVG(sub_rentals)   AS avg_subsequent_rentals
FROM (
        SELECT a.customer_id,
               f.rating                         AS first_rating,
               a.total_spent,
               (a.total_rentals - 1)           AS sub_rentals
        FROM (
                SELECT p.customer_id,
                       SUM(p.amount)               AS total_spent,
                       COUNT(DISTINCT p.rental_id) AS total_rentals,
                       MIN(p.payment_date)         AS first_payment_date
                FROM payment AS p
                GROUP BY p.customer_id
             ) AS a
        JOIN payment  AS p1 ON p1.customer_id = a.customer_id
                           AND p1.payment_date = a.first_payment_date
        JOIN rental   AS r  ON r.rental_id    = p1.rental_id
        JOIN inventory AS i ON i.inventory_id = r.inventory_id
        JOIN film      AS f ON f.film_id      = i.film_id
     )
GROUP BY first_rating
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 10. FINAL ANSWER: average total spending and average number of subsequent rentals
--                   for customers grouped by the rating of their first rented movie
SELECT
       f.rating                                               AS rating,
       ROUND(AVG(cust_stats.total_spent), 2)                  AS avg_total_amount_spent,
       ROUND(AVG(cust_stats.total_rentals - 1), 2)            AS avg_subsequent_rentals
FROM (
        -- customer-level totals & first-payment date
        SELECT  p.customer_id,
                SUM(p.amount)               AS total_spent,
                COUNT(DISTINCT p.rental_id) AS total_rentals,
                MIN(p.payment_date)         AS first_payment_date
        FROM payment AS p
        GROUP BY p.customer_id
     ) AS cust_stats
JOIN payment   AS fp ON  fp.customer_id = cust_stats.customer_id
                    AND fp.payment_date = cust_stats.first_payment_date   -- the first payment row
JOIN rental    AS r  ON r.rental_id    = fp.rental_id
JOIN inventory AS i  ON i.inventory_id = r.inventory_id
JOIN film      AS f  ON f.film_id      = i.film_id
GROUP BY f.rating
ORDER BY f.rating;
```

Each successive query uncovers another layer of information until the last statement returns, for every rating category of the customers’ first‑ever rented film,  
• the average total amount they eventually spent, and  
• the average count of rentals they made after that first one.
